Privately Attack Chains =E2=80=94 defensive synthesis 1 NEW ATTACK CHAINS
AC-2026-AGENT-CONFUSED-DEPUTY-REPO-EXFIL =E2=80=94 LAB-VALIDATED

Canonical fingerprint: untrusted instruction -> privileged enterprise agent
-> attacker-supplied legitimate destination identity -> independent policy
checks pass -> sensitive source read -> external destination write.

Mandiant reporting described an enterprise red-team assessment where
testers manipulated an internal AI assistant that legitimately managed
repositories and CI/CD. They supplied a token for an external repository
they controlled; because GitHub was an approved destination, the agent
cloned sensitive internal repositories and pushed them to that external
account.

Stages: attacker-controlled instruction [CONFIRMED] -> trusted internal
agent [CONFIRMED] -> supplied external identity/token [CONFIRMED] -> agent
accepts claimed authorization context [CONFIRMED] -> legitimate internal
repository authority [CONFIRMED] -> approved-domain policy passes GitHub
destination [CONFIRMED] -> agent clones internal repositories [CONFIRMED]
-> pushes data to controlled repository [CONFIRMED] -> persistence/cashout
[N/A/UNKNOWN].

Mechanism: agent has legitimate source authority + attacker supplies
legitimate destination authority + security policy validates each
independently but never validates the relationship between them.
2 MATERIAL UPDATE =E2=80=94 supply-chain access reaches AI credentials/data

Mandiant reporting on UNC6780/TeamPCP supply-chain compromises described
theft of AI-service credentials and proprietary AI data alongside
prompt-injection activity against coding assistants and LLM-based security
scanners.

Updated chain: developer/supply-chain compromise [CONFIRMED] ->
developer/AI environment [CONFIRMED] -> AI-service credential access
[CONFIRMED] -> proprietary AI-data access [CONFIRMED] -> further service
authority [UNKNOWN].
3-8 SYNTHESIS

Key recurring mechanism: relationship-blind authorization. Every individual
operation can be authorized while the composition is unauthorized.

QP implication: ALLOW(read X) + ALLOW(write Y) must not imply ALLOW(copy X
-> Y). Authority needs provenance and flow constraints, not only capability
ACLs.

Detection: correlate source sensitivity x destination ownership x
initiating instruction provenance; internal repository reads followed by
writes to newly introduced external identities; externally supplied
credentials introduced during agent sessions; cross-organization repository
transfers; changed authorization claims immediately before data movement.

Safe lab: two disposable repositories, legitimate read access to A, fake
token granting write access to B, harmless marker file; test whether
natural-language authorization claims cause transfer.
9 JSONL DELTA

